1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,480
Part 2 opens the image we built;
this scene and the next are extras.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,046
First, docker image ls:
518 megabytes on disk,

3
00:00:09,046 --> 00:00:14,123
and 144 megabytes of content,
at the time of recording.

4
00:00:14,443 --> 00:00:18,464
Content is the compressed layers
that travel to a registry;

5
00:00:18,464 --> 00:00:20,373
disk adds the unpacked copy.

6
00:00:21,617 --> 00:00:24,817
Then docker history lists the layers,
newest first.

7
00:00:25,137 --> 00:00:29,884
Read it bottom-up,
and it's the Dockerfile top-down:

8
00:00:29,884 --> 00:00:33,444
the apt and pip step at 97.5 megabytes,

9
00:00:33,444 --> 00:00:38,556
our code at 250 kilobytes,
and uv sync at 125 megabytes.

10
00:00:38,876 --> 00:00:44,251
The ENV, ENTRYPOINT and CMD rows
are zero bytes: config only.

11
00:00:46,133 --> 00:00:51,823
Below them sit the base image's own rows:
Debian bookworm at the bottom,

12
00:00:51,823 --> 00:00:56,565
the PATH that puts /usr/local/bin first,
and Python 3.12.15.

13
00:00:59,967 --> 00:01:05,236
To look yourself, start a container again;
these are from our second run in part 1.

14
00:01:05,556 --> 00:01:12,020
There, python is 3.15.0,
while the base Python is still 3.12.15.

15
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:18,697
Most of that 125 megabyte layer
is this second Python, in uv's own folder.

16
00:01:18,933 --> 00:01:20,533
Now list the top folder.

17
00:01:20,853 --> 00:01:24,270
Beside the system folders
sit the hidden git folder,

18
00:01:24,270 --> 00:01:28,213
the editor's VS Code folder,
the README and your Dockerfile.

19
00:01:28,533 --> 00:01:31,372
The COPY line copied
the whole build context,

20
00:01:31,372 --> 00:01:33,013
your git history included.

21
00:01:33,333 --> 00:01:35,595
Type exit to get back to your laptop.

22
00:01:35,833 --> 00:01:39,438
That's a gap in the tutorial:
there's no .dockerignore file.

23
00:01:39,758 --> 00:01:45,092
In your own projects, add one listing .git,
.venv and your secrets folder.

24
00:01:45,412 --> 00:01:48,740
It matters most once the image is public,
and that's soon.

25
00:01:49,783 --> 00:01:54,050
So why did COPY and uv sync rerun
when we only added a line?

26
00:01:54,370 --> 00:01:56,418
Docker caches every build step.

27
00:01:56,738 --> 00:02:01,175
A step is reused when its parent step
and its instruction are unchanged.

28
00:02:01,495 --> 00:02:04,951
For COPY, the checksum
of the copied files must match too.

29
00:02:05,271 --> 00:02:09,687
The first step that changes runs again,
and so does every step after it.

30
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,331
Rebuild with nothing changed,
adding the extra flag for the full log.

31
00:02:14,651 --> 00:02:15,995
FROM reuses the base,

32
00:02:15,995 --> 00:02:19,706
and the three steps after it all say CACHED:
nothing runs.

33
00:02:19,950 --> 00:02:20,995
Now predict.

34
00:02:21,315 --> 00:02:24,878
You change one word in cli.py and rebuild.

35
00:02:25,198 --> 00:02:27,054
Which of the four steps run again?

36
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:40,977
Try it, as an extra: on line 49,
change I love cheese to I love brie.

37
00:02:41,297 --> 00:02:43,093
The git diff shows the change;

38
00:02:43,093 --> 00:02:45,905
the build context now
has a different checksum.

39
00:02:46,150 --> 00:02:47,150
Rebuild.

40
00:02:47,345 --> 00:02:49,777
FROM and the apt and pip step are reused.

41
00:02:50,097 --> 00:02:54,173
But the checksum of the copied files
changed, so COPY runs,

42
00:02:54,173 --> 00:02:57,350
and uv sync after it,
Python download and all.

43
00:02:57,600 --> 00:02:59,840
Now undo the edit with git checkout.

44
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:02,485
Same files, so the cache comes back?

45
00:03:02,805 --> 00:03:05,301
No: COPY and uv sync run again.

46
00:03:05,621 --> 00:03:09,612
Git rewrote its own index file,
inside the hidden git folder,

47
00:03:09,612 --> 00:03:12,490
and that folder is in the build context too.

48
00:03:12,733 --> 00:03:15,016
That answers the question from part 1.

49
00:03:15,336 --> 00:03:18,191
Your Dockerfile sits
in the build context too,

50
00:03:18,191 --> 00:03:20,797
so every edit to it changed that checksum.

51
00:03:21,117 --> 00:03:24,466
While you follow the README step by step,
that's expected.

52
00:03:24,716 --> 00:03:28,046
In your own projects,
put the slow steps first:

53
00:03:28,046 --> 00:03:32,439
copy only the files uv sync needs,
run it, then copy the rest.

54
00:03:32,759 --> 00:03:37,601
A .dockerignore keeps the git folder,
any local venv and the Dockerfile out.

55
00:03:37,921 --> 00:03:42,551
Now an edit to cli.py reruns
only the last COPY.

56
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:48,576
Now the README section
Pushing Docker Image to Docker Hub.

57
00:03:48,896 --> 00:03:54,720
First, sign up and create an access token,
with read and write access, not the default.

58
00:03:55,040 --> 00:03:59,541
Copy it right away: it's shown only once,
and it works like a password.

59
00:03:59,861 --> 00:04:01,269
Pause and make yours.

60
00:04:01,517 --> 00:04:06,957
Next, log in: docker login,
-u with your user name, -p with your token.

61
00:04:07,277 --> 00:04:09,666
We have no account, so we couldn't run it.

62
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:14,344
So some steps use a stand-in,
a small registry on our own machine:

63
00:04:14,344 --> 00:04:15,489
just watch those.

64
00:04:15,809 --> 00:04:20,396
Logging in to it warns that a password
on the command line is insecure.

65
00:04:20,633 --> 00:04:25,966
That's a small slip in the README: with -p,
your token stays in your shell history.

66
00:04:26,286 --> 00:04:29,819
Instead, type docker login,
-u, and your user name,

67
00:04:29,819 --> 00:04:32,174
then paste the token when it asks.

68
00:04:32,494 --> 00:04:33,796
Pause and log in.

69
00:04:34,033 --> 00:04:35,847
Then tag the image:

70
00:04:35,847 --> 00:04:41,862
docker tag simple-translate
<USER NAME>/simple-translate-ac215.

71
00:04:42,182 --> 00:04:44,209
Pause and run it with your own name.

72
00:04:44,529 --> 00:04:49,649
An extra docker image ls shows two names,
one ID: a second name, not a copy.

73
00:04:49,883 --> 00:04:55,190
Read the long name in four parts:
the registry, docker.io, the default;

74
00:04:55,190 --> 00:04:58,928
your account; the repository;
and the tag, latest.

75
00:04:59,248 --> 00:05:02,854
Names without an account,
like our first, fall under library.

76
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:05,767
Then push: docker push, with the same name.

77
00:05:06,087 --> 00:05:07,239
Pause and run it.

78
00:05:07,559 --> 00:05:11,249
Yours should upload each layer
and end with a digest line.

79
00:05:11,569 --> 00:05:15,836
Without an account, Docker Hub refused ours:
push access denied.

80
00:05:16,156 --> 00:05:17,448
If you see it, log in,

81
00:05:17,448 --> 00:05:21,617
and check your token can write
and the name starts with your user name.

82
00:05:21,867 --> 00:05:25,591
On the stand-in, the push goes through,
ending with a digest:

83
00:05:25,591 --> 00:05:27,605
a fingerprint of the whole image.

84
00:05:27,850 --> 00:05:32,287
Push again: every layer already exists,
and only missing layers travel.

85
00:05:32,607 --> 00:05:37,399
On Docker Hub, even the base layers
may say Mounted from library/python:

86
00:05:37,399 --> 00:05:38,730
it already has them.

87
00:05:38,967 --> 00:05:43,447
And remember: a new Docker Hub repository
is public by default.

88
00:05:43,767 --> 00:05:46,156
Anyone can pull it, git folder and all.

89
00:05:48,483 --> 00:05:52,088
One more check before the VM;
this scene is all extras.

90
00:05:52,408 --> 00:05:58,126
Our machine is Intel or AMD,
and the image says linux/amd64.

91
00:05:58,446 --> 00:06:01,731
On an Apple Silicon Mac, you'd see arm64.

92
00:06:01,966 --> 00:06:03,225
Why does that matter?

93
00:06:03,545 --> 00:06:11,361
Every program in an image, bash, Python, uv,
is machine code for one kind of CPU:

94
00:06:11,361 --> 00:06:16,089
amd64 for Intel and AMD,
arm64 for Apple Silicon.

95
00:06:16,409 --> 00:06:19,813
A container's programs
run directly on the CPU,

96
00:06:19,813 --> 00:06:25,027
so unless an emulator steps in,
the CPU must speak the image's language.

97
00:06:25,347 --> 00:06:29,785
Docker Desktop has one built in;
a plain Linux VM doesn't.

98
00:06:30,033 --> 00:06:33,297
A tag can point at a menu,
one image per platform.

99
00:06:33,617 --> 00:06:40,294
This python tag lists five platforms:
amd64, two kinds of arm, and two rarer ones.

100
00:06:40,614 --> 00:06:43,196
Docker pulls the row
that matches your machine.

101
00:06:43,433 --> 00:06:49,183
The instructor's image,
dlops/simple-translate, is no menu:

102
00:06:49,183 --> 00:06:51,326
one image, amd64 only.

103
00:06:51,566 --> 00:06:54,283
Our stand-in push is a small menu:

104
00:06:54,283 --> 00:06:59,395
amd64, plus an attestation,
a record of how the image was built.

105
00:06:59,715 --> 00:07:05,134
Built on an M-series Mac,
the amd64 row would say arm64.

106
00:07:08,383 --> 00:07:09,407
Now ponder.

107
00:07:09,727 --> 00:07:12,074
You built on an M-series Mac and pushed.

108
00:07:12,394 --> 00:07:16,148
Your VM is an ordinary Intel or AMD machine.

109
00:07:16,468 --> 00:07:18,004
Will your image run there?

110
00:07:26,850 --> 00:07:30,848
No. Here's the same failure
the other way around,

111
00:07:30,848 --> 00:07:34,615
on our machine, which has no emulator:
exec format error.

112
00:07:34,935 --> 00:07:37,730
Docker Desktop would just run it, emulated.

113
00:07:38,050 --> 00:07:43,724
A Mac-built image on a default VM fails
like this, or with no matching manifest.

114
00:07:44,566 --> 00:07:46,742
Two fixes, neither in the README.

115
00:07:47,062 --> 00:07:54,742
One: build for the VM's platform,
with the flag --platform=linux/amd64.

116
00:07:55,062 --> 00:07:58,859
It's the commented-out
line 12 of docker-shell.sh.

117
00:07:59,179 --> 00:08:02,507
On a Mac, it runs emulated:
slower, but it works.

118
00:08:02,827 --> 00:08:05,004
Then tag and push again, as before.

119
00:08:05,250 --> 00:08:08,178
Two: docker-build-push.sh uses buildx,

120
00:08:08,178 --> 00:08:13,186
Docker's multi-platform builder,
to build both and push one menu.

121
00:08:16,500 --> 00:08:18,963
Put your user name on line 11,

122
00:08:18,963 --> 00:08:23,561
and simple-translate-ac215 on line 7,
the README's name.

123
00:08:23,881 --> 00:08:26,207
Log in first, then run it with bash.

124
00:08:26,527 --> 00:08:30,438
Afterwards,
switch back with docker buildx use default,

125
00:08:30,438 --> 00:08:32,926
or desktop-linux on Docker Desktop.

126
00:08:33,967 --> 00:08:38,063
The last README section:
Running App on VM using Docker.

127
00:08:38,383 --> 00:08:41,135
First, create a VM, as in lecture 1.

128
00:08:41,455 --> 00:08:43,204
The README gives no settings.

129
00:08:43,524 --> 00:08:48,561
An ordinary E2 machine
with Debian 12 or 13 will do;

130
00:08:48,561 --> 00:08:50,692
E2 machines are amd64.

131
00:08:51,012 --> 00:08:56,602
The meter starts: the VM bills by the second
while it runs, its disk while it exists.

132
00:08:56,867 --> 00:08:58,382
Next, SSH in.

133
00:08:58,702 --> 00:09:03,843
Pause now: create your VM,
and wait until its SSH terminal is open.

134
00:09:04,100 --> 00:09:08,004
Then install Docker:
sudo apt install docker.io.

135
00:09:08,324 --> 00:09:09,476
Pause and run it.

136
00:09:09,796 --> 00:09:10,948
In our stand-in,

137
00:09:10,948 --> 00:09:14,550
a fresh Debian with empty package lists,
it fails:

138
00:09:14,550 --> 00:09:16,324
Unable to locate package.

139
00:09:16,644 --> 00:09:19,695
If yours installed,
skip ahead to the version check.

140
00:09:19,950 --> 00:09:21,779
A small gap in the README:

141
00:09:21,779 --> 00:09:25,859
this section skips sudo apt-get update,
used in lecture 1.

142
00:09:26,179 --> 00:09:28,951
Without it, the package lists may be empty,

143
00:09:28,951 --> 00:09:31,982
as here, or out of date,
with Not Found errors.

144
00:09:32,302 --> 00:09:35,481
Run the update first,
an extra, and pause after it.

145
00:09:35,734 --> 00:09:43,683
Now the install works: 92 new packages,
a 107 megabyte download, on Debian 12;

146
00:09:43,683 --> 00:09:48,065
91 packages and 118 megabytes on Debian 13.

147
00:09:48,385 --> 00:09:49,409
Answer Y.

148
00:09:49,729 --> 00:09:50,880
Pause and run it.

149
00:09:51,150 --> 00:09:53,475
Then sudo docker --version.

150
00:09:53,795 --> 00:09:57,591
This is Debian's own Docker package:

151
00:09:57,591 --> 00:10:04,867
20.10.24 on Debian 12, 26.1.5 on Debian 13,
at the time of recording.

152
00:10:05,187 --> 00:10:07,428
Older than the laptop's, but plenty.

153
00:10:07,684 --> 00:10:11,161
Here Docker runs right
on the VM's Linux kernel;

154
00:10:11,161 --> 00:10:15,001
there's no hidden VM,
unlike Docker Desktop on a Mac.

155
00:10:15,267 --> 00:10:16,419
And why sudo?

156
00:10:16,739 --> 00:10:19,630
Without it, this extra docker ps is refused:

157
00:10:19,630 --> 00:10:24,888
permission denied on the Docker socket,
the door to Docker's background service.

158
00:10:25,208 --> 00:10:27,982
Only root and the docker group may open it.

159
00:10:29,417 --> 00:10:31,187
Then the deploy:

160
00:10:31,187 --> 00:10:36,500
sudo docker run --rm -ti
dlops/simple-translate.

161
00:10:36,820 --> 00:10:37,972
Pause and run it.

162
00:10:38,292 --> 00:10:42,811
The image isn't on this VM yet,
so Docker pulls it from Docker Hub,

163
00:10:42,811 --> 00:10:45,374
seven layers, then starts a container.

164
00:10:45,617 --> 00:10:49,651
Now run the app with python cli.py
and its defaults:

165
00:10:49,651 --> 00:10:52,444
I love cheese comes back in Italian.

166
00:10:52,764 --> 00:10:53,916
Pause and run it.

167
00:10:54,236 --> 00:10:58,801
No clone, no pip, no uv on this VM:
just Docker and one image.

168
00:10:59,050 --> 00:11:05,450
And the opening question:
why 3.14.7, when the recipe says 3.12?

169
00:11:05,770 --> 00:11:07,861
The same reason as in our build.

170
00:11:08,181 --> 00:11:13,406
This image was built
on September 16 with uv 0.12.15,

171
00:11:13,406 --> 00:11:19,914
and the newest stable Python
that uv knew in the range was 3.14.7.

172
00:11:20,234 --> 00:11:23,221
Our build today got 3.15.0.

173
00:11:23,467 --> 00:11:26,454
Same Dockerfile, a month apart, two Pythons.

174
00:11:26,774 --> 00:11:31,351
Nothing in the recipe is pinned:
pip installs the latest uv,

175
00:11:31,351 --> 00:11:35,521
the Python range is open,
and the repo has no uv.lock,

176
00:11:35,521 --> 00:11:37,760
so each build writes its own.

177
00:11:38,080 --> 00:11:41,984
An image freezes all that:
ship the image, not the recipe.

178
00:11:42,234 --> 00:11:43,279
Type exit.

179
00:11:43,599 --> 00:11:45,197
One more small mismatch:

180
00:11:45,197 --> 00:11:48,460
the README promises
to pull the image you pushed,

181
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:51,023
but this command runs the instructor's.

182
00:11:51,343 --> 00:11:55,930
Your image
is <USER NAME>/simple-translate-ac215.

183
00:11:56,250 --> 00:12:00,538
Run it with the same command,
once it's built for amd64.

184
00:12:01,734 --> 00:12:06,677
Also, an extra check shows
the published image lags the repo:

185
00:12:06,677 --> 00:12:11,377
its cli.py is the older one,
without raise_exception=True.

186
00:12:11,697 --> 00:12:16,241
If Google refuses a request,
that version quietly prints your input back.

187
00:12:16,561 --> 00:12:18,288
That's not a Docker problem.

188
00:12:18,534 --> 00:12:21,935
And if pulling the image fails
with toomanyrequests:

189
00:12:21,935 --> 00:12:25,401
Docker Hub limits how
often anonymous users can pull,

190
00:12:25,401 --> 00:12:28,475
per network address,
which a classroom can hit.

191
00:12:28,795 --> 00:12:32,592
Wait a while, or log in first,
with sudo docker login.

192
00:12:33,833 --> 00:12:35,476
Before you go, clean up.

193
00:12:35,796 --> 00:12:38,936
An extra check, sudo docker ps -a,

194
00:12:38,936 --> 00:12:43,369
shows no containers left behind,
thanks to --rm.

195
00:12:43,633 --> 00:12:47,537
Last README step:
delete the VM, with its boot disk,

196
00:12:47,537 --> 00:12:51,590
then check that VM instances
and Disks are both empty.

197
00:12:51,910 --> 00:12:53,254
Pause and do it now.

198
00:12:53,500 --> 00:12:54,908
Two optional chores.

199
00:12:55,228 --> 00:13:00,455
Your Docker Hub repository stays public
until you delete it or make it private.

200
00:13:00,775 --> 00:13:06,321
And to reclaim the space your images take
on the laptop, run docker system prune -a.

201
00:13:09,566 --> 00:13:12,809
Lecture 1 gave us a VM with a venv inside.

202
00:13:13,129 --> 00:13:19,038
Today a container slid in between:
the venv now lives in an image, at /.venv.

203
00:13:19,358 --> 00:13:22,750
You built it on your laptop,
pushed it to a registry,

204
00:13:22,750 --> 00:13:25,054
and ran it on a VM with one command.

205
00:13:25,633 --> 00:13:27,638
Five questions to test yourself.

206
00:13:27,958 --> 00:13:33,996
One: what's the difference between an image
and a container, and what does --rm delete?

207
00:13:34,316 --> 00:13:37,422
Two: what does our container run at startup,

208
00:13:37,422 --> 00:13:38,569
and what happens

209
00:13:38,569 --> 00:13:41,868
if you add python cli.py
after the image name?

210
00:13:42,116 --> 00:13:50,095
Three: the Dockerfile says Python 3.12,
so why does python report 3.15, or 3.14?

211
00:13:50,415 --> 00:13:52,954
Four: you edit one line of cli.py;

212
00:13:52,954 --> 00:13:57,433
which steps rerun,
and how would you reorder the Dockerfile?

213
00:13:57,753 --> 00:14:02,554
Five: name two ways to make
a Mac-built image run on a default VM.

214
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:07,493
The answers are in the exercises,
and every command is in the study guide.

215
00:14:07,813 --> 00:14:11,828
Next time, Containers 2:
five containers working together,

216
00:14:11,828 --> 00:14:14,390
a working directory, a non-root user,

217
00:14:14,390 --> 00:14:17,989
and your laptop's folder
mounted inside a container.

